{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>Her ways are ways of pleasantness, and all her paths are peace (Proverbs 3:17).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, of blessed memory, informs us in this verse (Proverbs 3:17) that the foundation and essence of the Torah is peace. So too we find that the essence of the creation of the world is peace, and therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: all the works of creation were created in their stature, in their knowledge, in their form — as it is said (Genesis 2:1) ״וכל צבאם״. And it is known that the heavens were created first, and they are called שמים because they are אש and מים, and behold, these are two opposites; the pairing and joining of them is possible only by means of peace, as it is written (Job 25:2) ״עושה שלום במרומיו״. The Holy One, blessed be He, is called Peace, as it is said (Judges 6:24) ״ויקרא לו ה' שלום״. And it is written (Song of Songs 1:1) ״שיר השירים אשר לשלמה״ — a king whose the peace is. He chose Israel over the seventy nations and called them שולמית, and this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Song of Songs 7:1): ״שובי שובי השולמית״ — a nation in whose midst the peace of the worlds dwells. And He gave them the Torah, whose entirety is peace, as it is said (Proverbs 3) ״דרכיה דרכי נועם וכל נתיבותיה שלום״. All the commandments of the Torah are peace for body and soul — peace for the body, as it is written (Exodus 15:26) ״ואמר אם שמוע תשמע לקול ה' אלהיך והישר בעיניו תעשה והאזנת למצותיו״ etc.; peace for the soul, for through the fulfillment of the commandments the pure soul returns to its root, as it is written (Psalms 19:8) ״תורת ה' תמימה משיבת נפש״, and the attribute of peace draws it onward. From this our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded in the Writings: when the wicked depart from the world, destroying angels go out to meet them and say to them (Isaiah 48:22) ״אין שלום אמר ה' לרשעים״. And great is the attribute of peace, for all the blessings are sealed with peace, and peace is the seal of all. Therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory enacted for us that the prayer should conclude with peace — המברך את עמו ישראל בשלום — and so they expounded in tractate Sukkah: the blessing of the Holy One, blessed be He, is peace, as it is said (Psalms 29:11) ״ה' יברך את עמו בשלום״. And it seems proper to say that it emerged for our Rabbis of blessed memory to seal the prayer with peace from the section of the sacrifices, for they established the prayers corresponding to the daily offerings, and the Torah, in the arrangement of the sections of the sacrifices, sealed for us with the shelamim sacrifice, as it is written (Leviticus 7:37) ״זאת התורה לעולה למנחה ולחטאת ולאשם ולמלואים ולזבח השלמים״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: why are they called shelamim? Because they instill peace in the world. And so we find that Solomon, of blessed memory, sealed Song of Songs with peace, as he said (Song of Songs 8:10) ״אז הייתי בעיניו כמוצאת שלום״. And likewise in Ecclesiastes, the times enumerated therein are sealed with peace, as it is said (Ecclesiastes 3:8) ״עת מלחמה ועת שלום״. And our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: even in a time of war one needs peace, as it is said (Deuteronomy 20:10) ״כי תקרב אל עיר להלחם עליה וקראת אליה לשלום״. Needless to say the lower world — for even those on high need peace, as it is said (Job 25:2) ״המשל ופחד עמו עושה שלום במרומיו״, and thus our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: המשל — this is Michael; ופחד — this is Gabriel; and the Holy One, blessed be He, makes peace between them. And is this not a fortiori: if in a place where there is no enmity and rivalry they need peace, in a place where there is enmity and rivalry, how much the more so! And the matter is that the expression עושה שלום במרומיו encompasses the peace of the heavens themselves, created and established from fire and water, and also encompasses the angels on high, to whom המשל ופחד alludes; and Michael is called המשל because he is the great prince, appointed and empowered to seek mercy for Israel. Needless to say the living — for even the dead need peace, as it is said (Genesis 15:15) ״ואתה תבוא אל אבותיך בשלום״. The attribute of peace was given to Aaron, and with it he merited to have life and peace (Malachi 2:5), and his seed merited an everlasting priesthood to bless Israel, as it is said (Numbers 6:26) ״וישם לך שלום״. From this you learn that peace is the sustenance of the world. And just as peace is the sustenance of the world, so is justice the sustenance of peace, for without justice people would rob and plunder and kill one another and the world would not endure; and through justice it endures, for thus our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: on three things the world stands — on judgment, on truth, and on peace, as it is said (Zechariah 8:16) ״אמת ומשפט שלום שפטו בשעריכם״, because judgments uphold the peace on which the entire world depends. Therefore we were commanded from the Torah to appoint courts in every gate, and this is what is written:" "<b>Judges and officers shall you appoint for yourself in all your gates, which YHWH your Elohim gives you, throughout your tribes; and they shall judge the people with righteous judgment.</b> The section of שופטים is juxtaposed to the section of the festivals, to inform us that even though Israel go up on pilgrimage three times a year, and there they find the priests, the Levites, and the teachers of Torah, and they can ask them about the Torah and the commandments and how to conduct themselves in them, and they will resolve for them every uncertainty — even so, a commandment is incumbent upon them to appoint judges in every city. And the judges are the sages knowledgeable in law and judgment who render the verdict, and the officers are those who enforce the verdict — these are the ones with staff and lash who go around the city and patrol its streets to straighten out every crooked thing and to rectify the measures and weights, and all this is by authority of the judges and their command." "<b>And know</b> that judges are of three categories: there is a court of seventy-one, there is a court of twenty-three, and a court of three. A court of three is a rabbinic enactment; they are established in a city that does not have a hundred and twenty Israelites. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: a court may not have fewer than three, so that there may be a majority and a minority if one of them disagrees. And they derived this from the verse, which mentioned Elohim three times (Exodus 22:7): ״ונקרב בעל הבית אל האלהים״ — ״עד האלהים״ — ״אשר ירשיעון אלהים״. And even though a single judge may judge according to Torah law, as it is said (Leviticus 19:15) ״בצדק תשפוט עמיתך״, it is a rabbinic enactment that there be three. And this court adjudicates monetary cases. And two who judged — their ruling is not a valid ruling. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: do not sit as a judge alone, for the only One who judges alone is One; but if the litigants accept him upon themselves as one of three, this is permitted. And they further expounded: Rabbi Yehudah ben Pazi said, even the Holy One, blessed be He, does not judge alone, as it is said (1 Kings 22:19) ״וכל צבא השמים עומדים עליו״ etc. — these incline to the right to acquit, and those to the left to convict. And even though He does not judge alone, He seals alone, as it is said (Daniel 10:21) ״אבל אגיד לך את הרשום בכתב אמת״. A court of twenty-three is from the Torah, and these are called the lesser Sanhedrin, and they are established in every city that has a hundred and twenty men of Israel or more, and they sit at the gate of the city, as it is said (Amos 5:15) ״והציגו בשער משפט״. And they adjudicate capital cases, and capital cases may not be adjudicated with fewer than twenty-three, from what is written (Numbers 35:25) ״ושפטו העדה והצילו העדה״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: עדה that judges — עדה that saves — ten convict and ten acquit, and they add three more so that the court not be evenly divided, and there be a majority to incline it. A court of seventy-one is from the Torah, and these are called the Great Sanhedrin; they were the ones fixed in the Temple and were called the Great Court in Jerusalem. This is from what is written (Numbers 11:16) ״אספה לי שבעים איש מזקני ישראל״, and with Moses, who was over them, as it is said (ibid.) ״והתיצבו שם עמך״ — behold seventy-one. The greatest in wisdom of all the seventy-one they seat as the head — he is the head of the academy, and among our Rabbis of blessed memory he is called Nasi, and he stood in the place of Moses. And the Av Beit Din was second to him and sat at his right, and all the rest of the Sanhedrin sat before the two of them, each according to his rank next to the other, and they sat in the shape of a half of a circular threshing floor, so that the Nasi and the Av Beit Din seated at his right could see them all." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> שפטים ושטרים — both of them need one another: if there is no judge there is no officer, if there is no officer there is no judge. Were it not for the awe of Yoav, David, of blessed memory, would not have been able to execute justice, as it is said (2 Samuel 8:15–16) ״ויהי דוד עושה משפט וצדקה לכל עמו ויואב בן צרויה על הצבא״, for David and Yoav judged as one: anyone who did not heed the judge, they would hand him over to Yoav, and he would extract from him by force. And so Job said (Job 29:16–17) ״אב אנכי לאביונים וריב לא ידעתי אחקרהו, ואשברה מתלעות עול ומשניו אשליך טרף״ — that is, even something edible that had been stolen he would extract from the mouth of the one obligated to pay. Great is the power of justice: when justice is done on earth, the Holy One, blessed be He, does not sit with them in judgment and judges them with the attribute of mercy; and when justice is not on earth, He judges them with the attribute of strict judgment. And so we find in the Midrash Tehillim: when justice is not on earth, justice is in heaven, and the Holy One, blessed be He, sits with them in judgment and exacts payment from them; but when justice is on earth, the Holy One, blessed be He, does not sit with them in judgment — for justice is the craft of the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is written (Isaiah 30:18) ״כי אלהי משפט ה'״, and when they take His craft and execute justice on earth, the Holy One, blessed be He, judges them with mercy. This is what is written (Leviticus 26:4) ״אם בחקותי תלכו ונתתי גשמיכם בעתם״." "<b>You shall appoint for yourself.</b> Moses risked his life for justice; therefore the judges are called by his name. Thus it is written (Exodus 2:11): ״וירא איש מצרי מכה איש עברי ויך את המצרי״. And likewise he risked his life for the Torah and for Israel — for the Torah, as it is written (Exodus 34:28): ״ויהי שם עם ה׳ ארבעים יום וארבעים לילה״; for Israel, as he said in the matter of the calf (Exodus 32:32): ״ועתה אם תשא חטאתם ואם אין מחני נא״ — and all are called by his name. The Torah is called by his name, as it is written (Malachi 3:22): ״זכרו תורת משה עבדי״; and Israel by his name (Exodus 32:7): ״כי שחת עמך״; and likewise the judges are called by his name, as it is written: ״תתן לך״." "<b>And they shall judge the people with righteous judgment.</b> Judgment of righteousness — such that the judgment is a decisive ruling. And thus it is written (Leviticus 19:15): ״בצדק תשפוט עמיתך״, and as they said: 'Let the judgment carve through the mountain.' And Rabbi Akiva said: 'There is no mercy in judgment.' Therefore it says משפט צדק, for the term משפט is the mean, an intermediate quality, and this is what is written (Psalms 112:5): ״יכלכל דבריו במשפט״, (Jeremiah 30:11): ״ויסרתיך למשפט״. And this is regarding the attributes of flesh and blood; but צדק as said of the attributes of the Holy One, blessed be He, includes both judgment and mercy. Therefore Scripture says (Psalms 99:4): ״משפט וצדקה ביעקב אתה עשית״, for משפט is the middle line, and it is the attribute of Jacob. And from this Scripture says (Jeremiah 10:24): ״יסרני ה׳ אך במשפט אל באפך פן תמעיטני״ — He mentioned אך to diminish the portion of judgment within it; therefore He said יסרני ה׳ אך במשפט, that is, in the portion of kindness; אל באפך, which is the portion of judgment — פן תמעיטני. And understand this well, for צדק is the attribute of the world's judgment, as it is written (Psalms 9:9): ״והוא ישפוט תבל בצדק״; it receives from the attribute of judgment above, which is called Elohim, and this is what is written (Psalms 58:2): ״אך יש אלהים שופטים בארץ״. And this is the secret of this verse: שופטים corresponds to Elohim, which is the attribute of judgment above, and שוטרים are Netzach and Hod, through whom actions go forth into the world, in word and its opposite, for good or for evil. And all is consummated through צדק, which is the end of the structure, and this is: ושפטו את העם משפט צדק. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: משפט צדק teaches that one performs charity (צדקה) with both of them — with the innocent one, in that he takes what is his, and with the liable one, in that they remove stolen property from his hand."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:49:38.684027556Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}